A||||5||Seanh||Steering Rack rebuilding ?'s||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-29-2000||11:13 PM||||During the rebuilding I want to get the steering rack taken care of, just so I dont have to later. My questions:<P>1. What all should be replaced for a rebuilding?<P>2. Does anyone sell a kit that includes everything you need to do it?<P>3. For the money, should I just buy a rebuilt one from the Fiero Store?<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||11-30-2000||08:03 AM||||I have been fighting with Advance Auto over their rebuilt racks from Cardone.  <P>The only replacable parts on a steering rack are the passenger side rack bushing and the inner tie rods.  In my experience, the pinion bearing seldom needs to be replaced, and the rack and pinion gear itself are not available.<P>The Cardone rebuit rack I got (several times) from Advance appeared to include new inner tie rods but they did nothing to the rack bushing.  The passenger side of the rack would move around in the bushing when you grab and shake it.  It appears that the Cardone racks are just cleaned and the housing is painted.  I don't thing they do anything about the rack bushing.<P>Rodney Dickman sells a replacement rack bushing for the 84-87 racks for less than $20.  To replace it you need to completely remove the rack from the housing, remove the retainer clip, and use a pipe or similar object to hammer the old bushing out of the rack housing.  It takes time to do this.  I would recommend a Helms manual which has good pictures of the process.  If your inner tie rods are in good shape, the rack bushing is really the only wear item to replace.<P>After the problems getting a good rack from Advance with the right number of turns (3 turns for the GT's), I just ordered a rebuilt rack from AutoZone for $115 plus core.  This includes new inner tie rods and is a much better deal than the Fiero Store (I don't think the Fiero store rack includes inner tie rods).  <P>I should pick up the new rack this evening.  I will let you know how it looks and if the rack bushing is tight in this one.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||William||11-30-2000||10:16 PM||||Did ya get to pick up that rack yet? When ya do, I have some questions.<P>1. Who makes that rack?<BR>2. Did it have the new bushings and are they metal or plastic?<BR>3. Was it a 3 turn unit and how do you confirm thats what you were getting? Did they list the 3 1/2 turn rack also.<P>When ya get the rack on I've got questions about how to center the rack so that the steering wheel will be straight when the coupling is attached.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||11-30-2000||10:29 PM||||I did pcik up one of the new racks I ordered from AutoZone and it looks much better than the rack I returned from Advance Auto.<P>The AutoZone rack is from a rebuilder called ASTCO or something like that.  AutoZone listed both a 3 turn and a 3 1/2 turn rack in their parts computer.  The rack bushing is very tight compared to the Cardone unit I returned.  <P>I will be installing it tomorrow evening or Saturday, but it looks much better than the units from Cardone<P>There really isn't any trick to centering the wheel.  The rack pinion shaft will only go on one way on the steering shaft coupler.  The wheel is actually centers by how the outer tie rods are adjusted during the alignment.  I eyeball it when I put things together but I always leave strick instructions with the alignment tech to get the wheel straight!<P>The pre 88 cars used a metal rack bushing.  I can't confirm this since I don't want to break the band holding the dust cover boot on the rack.  The 88 cars used a metal bushing early in the production run but it was changed to a plastic bushing later.  That is what GM sold a replacement bushings. Rodney Dickman sells a metal rach bushing for the pre-88's and the 88 racks.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||10:46 PM||||Hey, think you know anyone who could rebuild a rack?  i've got mine that needs to be rebuilt.  I don't wanna get a new one from toyota, because their price is outrageous.  would you belive $738??  <P>Thanks for the tips tho, I'll see about Advance and Autozone about a refurbed rack. <P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||12-01-2000||05:41 PM||||id defineatly get a fac rebuilt rack. too much work for a little savings, there around $150-199 here||||reg||1
